WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:21.860
I just came back from a community that holds a secret to human survival.

00:21.860 --> 00:29.400
It's a place where women run the show, have sex to say hello, and play rules a day.

00:29.400 --> 00:37.680
Where fun is serious business, and no, this isn't Burning Man or San Francisco.

00:37.680 --> 00:41.020
Ladies and gentlemen, meet your cousins.

00:41.020 --> 00:45.960
This is the world of wild bonobos in the jungles of Congo.

00:45.960 --> 00:52.160
Bonobos are, together with chimpanzees, your living closest relative.

00:52.160 --> 00:57.520
That means we all share a common ancestor, an evolutionary grandmother who lived around

00:57.520 --> 01:00.080
six million years ago.

01:00.080 --> 01:08.600
Now, chimpanzees are well known for their aggression.

01:08.600 --> 01:14.200
But unfortunately, we have made too much of an emphasis of this aspect in our narratives

01:14.200 --> 01:17.040
of human evolution.

01:17.040 --> 01:20.800
But bonobos show us the other side of the coin.

01:21.320 --> 01:29.240
While chimpanzees are dominated by big, scary guys, bonobos society is run by empowered

01:29.240 --> 01:31.840
females.

01:31.840 --> 01:38.320
These guys have really worked something out, since this leads to a highly tolerant society,

01:38.320 --> 01:43.520
where fatal violence has not been observed yet.

01:43.520 --> 01:48.640
But unfortunately, bonobos are the least understood of the great apes.

01:48.680 --> 01:55.360
They live in the depths of the Congolese jungle, and it has been very difficult to study them.

01:55.360 --> 02:03.000
Dear Congo is a paradox, a land of extraordinary biodiversity and beauty, but also the heart

02:03.000 --> 02:10.960
of darkness itself, the scene of a violent conflict that has raged for decades and claimed

02:10.960 --> 02:17.140
nearly as many lives as the First World War.

02:17.140 --> 02:23.180
Not surprisingly, this destruction also endangers bonobo survival.

02:23.180 --> 02:29.440
Bush mid-trade and forest loss means we couldn't fill a small stadium with all the bonobos

02:29.440 --> 02:35.140
that are left in the world, and we're not even sure of that, to be honest.

02:35.140 --> 02:43.820
Yet in this land of violence and chaos, you can hear hidden laughter swaying the trees.

02:43.820 --> 02:46.820
Who are these cousins?

02:46.820 --> 02:52.980
We know them as they make love, not war, apes, since they have frequent promiscuous

02:52.980 --> 02:58.420
and bisexual sex to manage conflict and solve social issues.

02:58.420 --> 03:05.400
Now, I'm not saying this is the solution to all of humanity's problems, since there's

03:05.400 --> 03:09.300
more to bonobo life than the Kama Sutra.

03:09.300 --> 03:13.100
Bonobos like humans love to play throughout their entire lives.

03:13.100 --> 03:15.560
Play is not just child's games.

03:15.560 --> 03:21.740
For us and them, play is foundational for bonding relationships and fostering tolerance.

03:21.740 --> 03:27.120
It's where we learn to trust and where we learn about the rules of the game.

03:27.120 --> 03:36.200
Play increases creativity and resilience, and it's all about the generation of diversity.

03:36.200 --> 03:42.320
Diversity of interactions, diversity of behaviors, diversity of connections.

03:42.320 --> 03:48.120
And when you watch bonobo play, you're seeing the very evolutionary roots of human laughter,

03:48.120 --> 03:51.320
dance, and ritual.

03:51.320 --> 03:54.480
Play is the glue that binds us together.

03:54.480 --> 04:01.040
Now, I don't know how you play, but I want to show you a couple of unique clips, fresh

04:01.040 --> 04:02.040
from the wild.

04:02.040 --> 04:05.560
First, it's a ballgame, bonobo style.

04:05.560 --> 04:08.000
And I do not mean football.

04:08.000 --> 04:14.560
So here, we have a young female and a male engaged in a chase game.

04:14.560 --> 04:16.660
Have a look what she's doing.

04:16.660 --> 04:21.600
It might be the evolutionary origin of the phrase, she's got him by the balls.

04:21.600 --> 04:28.760
Only that I think he's rather loving it here, right?

04:28.760 --> 04:31.760
Yeah.

04:31.760 --> 04:36.280
So sex play is common in both bonobos and humans.

04:36.280 --> 04:45.400
And this video is really interesting because it shows the inventiveness of bringing unusual

04:45.400 --> 04:53.480
elements into play, such as testicles, and also how play both requires trust and fosters

04:53.480 --> 04:58.360
trust, while at the same time being tremendous fun.

04:58.360 --> 05:09.840
But play is a shape-shifter, and it can take many forms, some of which are more quiet,

05:09.840 --> 05:14.440
imaginative, curious, maybe where wonder is discovered anew.

05:14.440 --> 05:20.360
And I want you to see, this is Fuku, a young female, and she's quietly playing with water.

05:20.360 --> 05:25.760
I think like her, we sometimes play alone, and we explore the boundaries of our inner

05:25.760 --> 05:28.840
and our outer worlds.

05:28.840 --> 05:34.680
And it's that playful curiosity that drives us to explore, drives us to interact.

05:34.680 --> 05:41.860
And then the unexpected connections we form are the real hotbed for creativity.

05:41.860 --> 05:49.520
So these are just small tasters into the insights that bonobos give us to our past and present.

05:49.520 --> 05:56.000
But they also hold a secret for our future, a future where we need to adapt to an increasingly

05:56.000 --> 06:01.560
challenging world through greater creativity and greater cooperation.

06:01.560 --> 06:07.040
The secret is that play is the key to these capacities.

06:07.040 --> 06:11.240
In other words, play is our adaptive wild card.

06:11.240 --> 06:18.720
In order to adapt successfully to a changing world, we need to play.

06:18.720 --> 06:22.360
But will we make the most of our playfulness?

06:22.360 --> 06:26.440
Play is not frivolous, play is essential.

06:26.440 --> 06:31.760
For bonobos and humans alike, life is not just red in tooth and claw.

06:31.760 --> 06:36.600
In times when it seems least appropriate to play, it might be the times when it's most

06:36.600 --> 06:38.740
urgent.

06:38.740 --> 06:47.220
And so, my fellow primates, let us embrace this gift from evolution and play together

06:47.220 --> 06:52.200
as we rediscover creativity, fellowship, and wonder.

06:52.200 --> 06:52.700
Thank you.

